# Extension environment template - DOCUMENTATION ONLY (Vite never loads .env.example).
# Copy the keys into the real, gitignored `.env` described below. Every VITE_* value is
# inlined into the bundle and readable by anyone who unzips the extension, so nothing secret can
# live here. That is fine for what is here: an OAuth client id is public by design.
#
# `.env` is read in every mode, so one file covers `pnpm ext:dev` and `pnpm ext:build` alike. That
# suits what lives here, because the values are the same in every build: `key` in wxt.config.ts pins
# the same Chromium extension id in dev and in a store release, and
# `browser_specific_settings.gecko.id` does the same on Firefox - so one OAuth client's redirect
# URIs cover both, and there is no dev-vs-prod client to keep apart. (`.env.local` still wins over
# `.env` if one ever exists, which is exactly how to override these locally.)
#
# Values are inlined when the build starts, so a running dev server will not see an edit here -
# restart it.
#
# WXT generates the `import.meta.env` types from whichever keys these files define, so a key added
# here without being added to `.env` will not typecheck. Run `pnpm postinstall` after editing.


# == .env ===================================================================================

# The "Use Google account" button in Identity Override. `identity.launchWebAuthFlow` sends the
# browser to Google's account chooser with this id, and Google answers with an ID token carrying
# the account's real `given_name` - which beats guessing a name out of an email address.
#
# A "Web application" client in the `cyberpunk-2077-new-tab-507422` Google Cloud project, with these
# two authorized redirect URIs and nothing else:
#
#   https://eeoblgdlmcmfnolbfjboemgknolcjgad.chromiumapp.org/                Chrome, Edge, Opera
#   https://e57b86a3bf88db48ebc8c147ac8fde55db3d98cb.extensions.allizom.org/ Firefox
#
# Scopes are `openid` and `profile`, both non-sensitive, so the app needs no Google review and the
# consent screen's App domain fields can stay empty. Leaving them empty does keep the app in
# "Testing" though - the console only enables "Publish app" once a homepage and a privacy policy URL
# are filled in - so every Google account that signs in has to be listed as a test user under
# Audience, up to a cap of 100.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-oauth-client.apps.googleusercontent.com

# The secret the console hands you next to that id. It is inlined into the bundle like everything
# else here, and that is inherent rather than sloppy: an extension is a public client, and Google's
# Web application type requires the secret at the token endpoint even alongside PKCE. What binds the
# flow is the pair of redirect URIs above, which only this extension can be sent to.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-oauth-client-secret

# The GA4 property that counts installs. Both values are inlined into the bundle like everything
# else here, and both are meant to be: the Measurement Protocol has no confidential half. An "API
# secret" is a write-only ticket for one property - it can send events and cannot read a single
# report - so the worst it buys someone who unzips the extension is the ability to post junk into
# your own property, which is why it is rotatable from the same screen that made it.
#
# Leave either blank and nothing is ever sent - `report()` returns before it builds a payload. That
# is what a fork or a local build gets by default.
#
# Analytics is off in a dev build, off on a sideloaded install, and off when the reader has opted
# out (`local:analyticsOptOut`) or Firefox was told no at install. A dev build that IS enabled talks
# to GA4's `/debug/mp/collect`, which validates the payload and answers with what is wrong with it
# instead of recording anything.
#
# Admin > Data Streams > (your web stream) > Measurement ID.
VITE_GA4_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-XXXXXXXXXX

# The same screen: Measurement Protocol API secrets > Create. One per property is enough.
VITE_GA4_API_SECRET=your-measurement-protocol-secret
